"""Central Asia and Oceania: Kazakhstan, Uzbekistan, Turkmenistan, Kyrgyzstan and Tajikistan; Australia, New Zealand,
Papua New Guinea, Hawaii, Fiji, Tonga and Samoa. The steppe peoples and the Kazakh Khanate are at home in Kazakhstan;
the Samanids, the Khwarazmshahs, Timur's empire and the khanates of Bukhara and Khiva in Uzbekistan, where their capitals
stood; the Karluks, the Karakhanids, the Kara Khitai and the Chagatai Khanate (Moghulistan) in Kyrgyzstan, around their
capital Balasagun and the Tian Shan; the Oghuz of the Seljuk age in Turkmenistan. Parthia, Bactria, the Kushans and the
Mongol Empire stay with Iran, Afghanistan and Mongolia. The map's "central Asian khanates" is a loose name that moves:
Mongolia's in 1600-1715 (the Khalkha and the Dzungars), Kazakhstan's in 1783-1815 (the three juzes) and Uzbekistan's
after that (Bukhara, Khiva and their neighbours under Russia). The many Aboriginal nations drawn on the 1600-1815 maps
stay as themselves, like the Native nations of the Americas."""
from _lib import L, P

# ---------- Central Asia ----------

L('kazakhstan', 'Kazakhstan', 'Central Asia / Steppe', (71.43, 51.13),
  P('Botai and the Bronze Age steppe', -4000, -900, 'Andronovo', 'Karasuk culture', short='Bronze Age',
    note='Around 3500 BC the Botai people of the northern steppe kept herds of horses, among the first people known to have done so. In the Bronze Age the herders and copper miners of the Andronovo culture, probably early speakers of Indo-Iranian languages, spread across the whole steppe.'),
  P('Saka', -900, -160, '~Scythians',
    note='Saka (Scythian) horse nomads ruled the steppe and buried their chiefs in great mounds; the "Golden Man" of the Issyk kurgan near Almaty wore a suit of 4,000 gold plates. Herodotus tells how Tomyris, queen of the Massagetae, beat and killed Cyrus the Great of Persia in 530 BC.'),
  P('Wusun, Kangju and the Huns', -160, 552, 'Saces', 'Yueban', '~Scythians', short='Wusun & Kangju',
    note='The Wusun ruled the Ili valley and the Kangju the Syr Darya, and both traded and fought with Han China. Yueban, a remnant of the northern Xiongnu, held the steppe north of the Wusun into the 400s, and the Huns crossed the land on their way west.'),
  P('Turkic khaganates', 552, 766, '~Göktürks', '~Western Gokturk Khaganate', short='Turkic khaganates',
    note='Bumin Qaghan founded the Turkic Khaganate in 552, and the Western Turks ruled the steppe from Suyab and Talas. In 751 the Abbasids and their Karluk allies beat a Tang Chinese army on the Talas river; legend has it that Chinese prisoners taught the Arabs to make paper.'),
  P('Oghuz, Kimeks and Karluks', 766, 1050, 'Oghuz Turks', 'Kimek-Kipchak khaganate', 'Peshemegs', '~Karluks',
    '~Khazars', short='Oghuz & Kimeks',
    note='The Oghuz ruled the lower Syr Darya from Yangikent, the Kimeks the Irtysh and the Karluks the southeast, while the Pechenegs roamed the west. Islam spread from the oasis towns; the philosopher al-Farabi, the "Second Teacher" after Aristotle, probably came from Farab (Otrar). The Seljuks, who conquered Persia, were Oghuz from here.'),
  P('Kipchak steppe', 1050, 1219, 'Cuman-Kipchak confederation', 'Cuman Khanates', '~Karkhanids',
    '~Kara Khitai Khaganate', '~Khwarazmian dynasty', '~Oghuz', short='Kipchaks',
    note='Kipchak (Cuman) tribes took the steppe, which Persian writers called the Desht-i Kipchak, the Kipchak plain, from the Irtysh to the Danube. The Karakhanids and then the Kara Khitai held the southern towns, and in 1218 the Khwarazmshah\'s governor of Otrar killed a caravan of Genghis Khan\'s merchants.'),
  P('Mongol rule and the White Horde', 1219, 1465, 'White Horde', '~Mongol Empire', '~Khanate of the Golden Horde',
    '~Golden Horde', '~Chagatai Khanate', '~Timurid Empire', short='Mongol rule',
    note='Genghis Khan razed Otrar in 1219–20, and the steppe passed to his son Jochi\'s heirs, the Golden Horde in the west and the White Horde in the east. Timur built the great shrine of Khoja Ahmed Yasawi at Turkistan, and died at Otrar in 1405.'),
  P('Kazakh Khanate', 1465, 1781, 'Quazaq Khanate', 'White Horde', 'Nogai Horde', '~Golden Horde', '~Chagatai Khanate',
    '~central Asian khanates',
    short='Kazakh Khanate',
    note='Kerei and Janibek founded the Kazakh Khanate in 1465; under Kasym Khan it counted a million people. The Dzungar invasion of 1723 began the "years of great disaster" and drove the Junior Juz, one of the three Kazakh hordes, to seek Russian protection in 1731; Ablai Khan reunited the hordes until 1781.'),
  P('The last khans', 1781, 1847, 'central Asian khanates', '~Russian Empire', short='Last khans',
    note='Russia advanced with lines of forts from the north and abolished the khans of the Middle Juz in 1822 and of the Junior Juz in 1824. Ablai\'s grandson Kenesary Kasymov, the last khan, fought Russia for ten years until he was killed in 1847.'),
  P('Russian rule', 1847, 1917, '~Russian Empire',
    note='Russia took the Senior Juz in the south, founded the fort of Verny (Almaty) in 1854 and gave the herders\' pastures to Russian and Ukrainian settlers. When the tsar ordered Central Asians to dig trenches behind the front in 1916, the Kazakhs and Kyrgyz rose, and Amangeldy Imanov besieged Turgai.'),
  P('Alash and Soviet Kazakhstan', 1917, 1991, '~USSR', short='Soviet',
    note='The Alash Orda briefly ruled an autonomous Kazakhstan. Collectivisation caused a famine in 1930–33 that killed about 1.5 million people, some 40 percent of the Kazakhs; Stalin deported whole peoples here, and the Soviets launched Gagarin from Baikonur in 1961 and set off 456 nuclear tests near Semipalatinsk.'),
  P('Republic of Kazakhstan', 1991, None, 'Kazakhstan', short='Republic',
    note='Kazakhstan was the last republic to leave the Soviet Union, in December 1991. Nursultan Nazarbayev ruled until 2019, gave up the nuclear weapons left on its soil and moved the capital north to Astana in 1997; protests in January 2022 ended in a deadly crackdown.'),
)

L('uzbekistan', 'Uzbekistan', 'Central Asia / Steppe', (66.96, 39.65),
  P('Oxus civilization', -4000, -1500, '~Oxus', '~Andronovo', short='Oxus',
    note='Neanderthals buried a child in the cave of Teshik-Tash some 70,000 years ago. In the Bronze Age the walled towns of the Oxus civilization, such as Sapalli Tepe, farmed the southern oases, while Andronovo herders roamed the north.'),
  P('Sogdiana and Khwarazm', -1500, -329, '~Achaemenid Empire', short='Sogdiana',
    note='Iranian peoples, the Sogdians around Samarkand (Afrasiab) and the Chorasmians on the lower Amu Darya, built oasis cities from the 700s BC; Zoroastrianism may have begun in this land or in neighbouring Bactria. Cyrus the Great made them Persian satrapies around 540 BC.'),
  P('Alexander and the Greco-Bactrians', -329, -130, '~Empire of Alexander', '~Seleucid Kingdom', '~Bactria',
    '~Scythians', short='Alexander',
    note='Alexander the Great took Marakanda (Samarkand) in 329 BC, fought the Sogdian rebel Spitamenes for two years and married Roxana, the daughter of a local lord. Greek kings of Bactria ruled the south after him.'),
  P('Kangju and the Kushans', -130, 350, '~Kushan Empire', '~Yuezhi', '~Scythians', '~Saces', short='Kushans',
    note='The Silk Road opened as Han China sent envoys west; in 104–101 BC Han armies marched to Fergana for its "heavenly horses". The Kangju ruled Sogdiana, and the Kushans the south, where Buddhist monasteries rose at Termez.'),
  P('Sogdian cities and the Turks', 350, 712, 'Kwarezm', '~Western Gokturk Khaganate', '~Göktürks', short='Sogdians',
    note='Sogdian merchants ran the Silk Road trade as far as China, and the kings of Samarkand covered their palace at Afrasiab with paintings of foreign envoys. The Hephthalites and then the Western Turks were their overlords, and the Afrighid kings ruled Khwarazm.'),
  P('Arab conquest', 712, 819, '~Umayyad Caliphate', '~Abbasid Caliphate', short='Arab conquest',
    note='Qutayba ibn Muslim took Bukhara in 709 and Samarkand and Khwarazm in 712, and Islam slowly took the place of the old faiths. Al-Khwarizmi, from Khwarazm, wrote the book that gave algebra its name, and "algorithm" comes from his own.'),
  P('Samanid Empire', 819, 999, 'Samanid Empire', short='Samanid',
    note='The Persian Samanids ruled from Bukhara, whose brick mausoleum of Ismail Samani still stands; their court revived Persian as a language of poetry, with Rudaki. Ibn Sina (Avicenna) was born near Bukhara in 980, and al-Biruni in Khwarazm in 973.'),
  P('Karakhanids and Khwarazmshahs', 999, 1220, 'Khwarazmian dynasty', '~Karkhanids', '~Kara Khitai Khaganate',
    '~Oghuz', '~Seljuk Empire', short='Karakhanids',
    note='The Turkic Karakhanids took Bukhara in 999 and built its Kalyan minaret in 1127; the Kara Khitai beat the Seljuk sultan near Samarkand in 1141. The Khwarazmshahs of Gurganj made the region the centre of an empire from the Aral Sea to the Persian Gulf, until Genghis Khan destroyed it.'),
  P('Mongols and the Chagatai Khanate', 1220, 1370, '~Mongol Empire', '~Chagatai Khanate', '~Ilkhanate',
    short='Mongols',
    note='Genghis Khan took Bukhara and Samarkand in 1220 and told the people of Bukhara that he was the punishment of God. The land became part of the khanate of his son Chagatai.'),
  P('Timurid Empire', 1370, 1500, 'Timurid Empire', '~Timurid Emirates', '~Chagatai Khanate', short='Timurid',
    note='Timur (Tamerlane), whose armies reached from Delhi to Ankara, made Samarkand his capital and filled it with blue-tiled buildings such as the Bibi-Khanym mosque and his tomb, the Gur-e-Amir. His grandson Ulugh Beg built an observatory there, and Babur, born in Andijan, went on to found the Mughal Empire in India.'),
  P('Khanates of Bukhara and Khiva', 1500, 1785, 'Bukara Khanate', 'Khiva Khanate', '~Quazaq Khanate',
    '~central Asian khanates',
    short='Bukhara & Khiva',
    note='Muhammad Shaybani\'s Uzbeks took Samarkand and Bukhara in 1500 and gave the country its name; their khans ruled from Bukhara, and another line from Khiva from 1511. The Sher-Dor and Tilya-Kori madrasas completed Samarkand\'s Registan, while the Kazakhs held Tashkent and a khanate of Kokand broke away in Fergana in 1709.'),
  P('Emirate of Bukhara, Khiva and Kokand', 1785, 1865, 'Turan', '~central Asian khanates', short='Three khanates',
    note='The Manghit emirs ruled Bukhara from 1785, and Kokand took Tashkent in 1809; the slave markets of Bukhara and Khiva sold captured Persians and Russians. Britain and Russia played their "Great Game" here, and the emir of Bukhara beheaded two British officers, Stoddart and Conolly, in 1842.'),
  P('Russian Turkestan', 1865, 1917, 'central Asian khanates', 'Bokhara Khanate', 'Khiva Khanate', 'Khanate of Kokand', '~Russian Empire', short='Russian',
    note='General Chernyayev stormed Tashkent in 1865, and it became the capital of Russian Turkestan; Bukhara (1868) and Khiva (1873) became Russian protectorates and Kokand was annexed in 1876. Railways reached Samarkand in 1888, and the land was turned over to cotton.'),
  P('Soviet Uzbekistan', 1917, 1991, '~USSR', short='Soviet',
    note='The Bolsheviks crushed the Kokand Autonomy in 1918 and ended the emir of Bukhara and the khan of Khiva in 1920; the Basmachi fought them for years. The Uzbek SSR was formed in 1924; irrigation for cotton drained the Aral Sea, and an earthquake levelled much of Tashkent in 1966.'),
  P('Republic of Uzbekistan', 1991, None, 'Uzbekistan', short='Republic',
    note='Uzbekistan became independent in 1991. Islam Karimov ruled it harshly until his death in 2016, and his troops killed hundreds of protesters at Andijan in 2005; Shavkat Mirziyoyev opened the country and ended the forced labour of the cotton harvest.'),
)

L('turkmenistan', 'Turkmenistan', 'Central Asia / Steppe', (58.38, 37.95),
  P('Jeitun, Namazga and Margiana', -4000, -1500, 'Namazga', '~Oxus', short='Namazga',
    note='Jeitun, near Ashgabat, was the first farming village in Central Asia, around 6000 BC. Towns such as Namazga and Altyn-Depe grew along the Kopet Dag, and Gonur Depe in the Murghab delta was a great centre of the Oxus civilization around 2000 BC.'),
  P('Margiana and Parthava', -1500, -330, '~Achaemenid Empire', short='Persian',
    note='Iranian peoples farmed the oasis of Margiana (Merv) and Parthava below the Kopet Dag; both became Persian satrapies, and Darius I crushed a revolt in Margiana in 521 BC.'),
  P('Alexander and the Seleucids', -330, -247, '~Empire of Alexander', '~Seleucid Kingdom', short='Seleucid',
    note='Alexander\'s men founded an Alexandria at Merv, and the Seleucids rebuilt it as Antiochia in Margiana.'),
  P('Parthian Empire', -247, 224, '~Parthia', '~Parthian Empire', '~Bactria', '~Kushan Empire', '~Scythians',
    short='Parthian',
    note='The Parni nomads under Arsaces founded the Parthian dynasty around 247 BC; their royal fortress of Nisa, near Ashgabat, held palaces, shrines and carved ivory drinking horns. Roman soldiers captured at Carrhae in 53 BC were sent to guard Merv.'),
  P('Sasanian Merv', 224, 651, '~Sasanian Empire', short='Sasanian',
    note='Merv was the Sasanians\' great frontier city, with Zoroastrian fire temples, Buddhist stupas and a Christian bishop; the last Sasanian king, Yazdegerd III, was murdered near Merv in 651.'),
  P('Arab caliphates', 651, 821, '~Umayyad Caliphate', '~Abbasid Caliphate', short='Arab',
    note='Merv became the Arab capital of Khorasan; Abu Muslim raised the black banners of the Abbasid revolution there in 747, and the caliph al-Ma\'mun ruled the empire from Merv until 818.'),
  P('Samanids, Ghaznavids and the Oghuz', 821, 1037, '~Samanid Empire', '~Oghuz Turks', '~Ghaznavid Emirate',
    short='Samanid',
    note='The Tahirids, the Samanids and the Ghaznavids ruled the oases in turn, while Oghuz Turks moved in from the north; the Muslims among them came to be called Turkmen.'),
  P('Seljuk Empire', 1037, 1157, 'Oghuz', '~Seljuk Empire', short='Seljuk',
    note='Oghuz Turkmen of the Seljuk clan took Merv in 1037 and beat the Ghaznavids at Dandanaqan in 1040, then conquered Persia. Merv, capital of Sultan Sanjar, may have been the largest city in the world around 1150; his mausoleum still stands.'),
  P('Khwarazmshahs and Mongols', 1157, 1370, '~Khwarazmian dynasty', '~Mongol Empire', '~Ilkhanate',
    '~Khanate of the Golden Horde', short='Mongols',
    note='The Khwarazmshahs ruled from Gurganj (Konye-Urgench). In 1221 Genghis Khan\'s son Tolui destroyed Merv, and chroniclers claimed he killed hundreds of thousands of people; the Ilkhans and the Golden Horde then held the land.'),
  P('Timurids', 1370, 1507, '~Timurid Empire', '~Timurid Emirates',
    note='Timur and his heirs ruled the oases, and his son Shah Rukh rebuilt Merv.'),
  P('Turkmen tribes', 1507, 1881, '~Safavid Empire', '~Khiva Khanate', '~central Asian khanates', '~Turan',
    '~Bokhara Khanate', short='Turkmen tribes',
    note='Turkmen tribes, the Teke, Yomut, Ersari and others, lived as herders and raiders between Safavid Persia, Khiva and Bukhara, selling captives in the slave markets; Magtymguly (1724–1807), the national poet, called them to unite. The Teke routed a Persian army at Merv in 1861.'),
  P('Russian Transcaspia', 1881, 1917, '~Russian Empire', '~central Asian khanates', short='Russian',
    note='General Skobelev stormed the Teke fortress of Geok Tepe in January 1881 and thousands of Turkmen were killed; Russia founded Ashgabat that year, took Merv in 1884 and ran a railway across the desert, while Khiva and Bukhara kept the north and east as Russian protectorates.'),
  P('Soviet Turkmenistan', 1917, 1991, '~USSR', short='Soviet',
    note='The Turkmen SSR was formed in 1924. An earthquake destroyed Ashgabat in 1948, killing tens of thousands of people; the Karakum Canal, dug from 1954, carried the Amu Darya\'s water into the desert, and a gas crater at Darvaza has been burning since about 1971.'),
  P('Independent Turkmenistan', 1991, None, 'Turkmenistan', short='Independent',
    note='Saparmurat Niyazov, "Turkmenbashi", ruled with a cult of his own person, renaming the months after himself and his mother and raising a golden statue that turned to face the sun; the UN recognised the country\'s permanent neutrality in 1995. Gurbanguly Berdimuhamedov followed him in 2006, and his son Serdar in 2022.'),
)

L('kyrgyzstan', 'Kyrgyzstan', 'Central Asia / Steppe', (74.6, 42.87),
  P('Bronze Age herders', -4000, -800, '~Andronovo', '~Karasuk culture', short='Bronze Age',
    note='Herders carved thousands of pictures of animals and hunters into the rocks of Saimaluu-Tash, high in the Tian Shan, from the Bronze Age on.'),
  P('Saka and Wusun', -800, 552, '~Scythians', '~Saces', short='Saka & Wusun',
    note='Saka nomads, then the Wusun, whose capital Chigu stood by Lake Issyk-Kul, grazed the mountain pastures. The Wusun were allies of Han China; the Han princess Liu Xijun, married to their old king around 105 BC, wrote a famous lament about her exile.'),
  P('Turkic khaganates', 552, 766, '~Göktürks', '~Western Gokturk Khaganate', short='Turkic khaganates',
    note='Suyab, in the Chuy valley, was the capital of the Western Turkic khagans and later of the Türgesh, and a Tang Chinese garrison town for forty years; the poet Li Bai was probably born there in 701.'),
  P('Karluks and Karakhanids', 766, 1130, 'Karluks', 'Karkhanids', short='Karakhanids',
    note='The Karluks took Suyab in 766. Then the Karakhanids, one of the first Turkic dynasties to become Muslim, ruled from Balasagun (with its Burana tower), Kashgar and Uzgen; Yusuf of Balasagun wrote the "Kutadgu Bilig" for them in 1069, the first great work of Turkic literature.'),
  P('Kara Khitai', 1130, 1218, 'Kara Khitai Khaganate',
    note='Yelü Dashi, a prince of the fallen Khitan Liao dynasty of China, took Balasagun in the 1130s and founded the Kara Khitai, who ruled Central Asia in the Chinese manner. The Naiman prince Kuchlug seized their throne in 1211, and the Mongols killed him in 1218.'),
  P('Mongols and Moghulistan', 1218, 1680, 'Chagatai Khanate', '~Mongol Empire', '~Timurid Empire', '~Bukara Khanate',
    '~Quazaq Khanate', short='Moghulistan',
    note='Balasagun surrendered to the Mongols in 1218, and the land passed to Chagatai\'s khanate and from 1347 to Moghulistan, its eastern half. The Kyrgyz, whose ancestors lived on the Yenisei in Siberia, became the main people of the Tian Shan by the 1500s and told their past in the epic of Manas.'),
  P('Dzungars and Kokand', 1680, 1876, '~central Asian khanates', '~Khanate of Kokand', '~Quazaq Khanate', short='Kokand',
    note='The Buddhist Oirat Dzungars raided and ruled the Kyrgyz until the Qing destroyed them in the 1750s; then the khans of Kokand took the valleys and built the fort of Pishpek (Bishkek) in 1825. Kurmanjan Datka, "the queen of the Alay", led the southern Kyrgyz.'),
  P('Russian rule', 1876, 1917, '~Russian Empire', '~central Asian khanates',
    note='Russia took the northern Kyrgyz lands in the 1860s and the south when it annexed Kokand in 1876, and settlers took the best pastures. In the revolt of 1916 tens of thousands of Kyrgyz were killed or died fleeing over the mountains to China, the Urkun.'),
  P('Soviet Kyrgyzstan', 1917, 1991, '~USSR', short='Soviet',
    note='The Soviets made the Kara-Kirghiz autonomous region in 1924 and the Kirghiz SSR in 1936, and renamed Pishpek Frunze. Chinghiz Aitmatov\'s novels made Kyrgyz life known around the world.'),
  P('Kyrgyz Republic', 1991, None, 'Kyrgyzstan', short='Republic',
    note='Kyrgyzstan became independent in 1991. Street revolutions overthrew its presidents in 2005 (the Tulip Revolution), 2010 and 2020, and hundreds of people, most of them Uzbeks, were killed in clashes around Osh in 2010.'),
)

L('tajikistan', 'Tajikistan', 'Central Asia / Steppe', (68.78, 38.56),
  P('Sarazm and the Oxus civilization', -4000, -1500, '~Oxus', short='Sarazm',
    note='Sarazm, near Panjakent, was one of the first towns in Central Asia, from about 3500 BC, trading turquoise and copper; the farmers of the Oxus civilization worked the southern valleys.'),
  P('Bactria and Sogdiana', -1500, -329, '~Achaemenid Empire', short='Bactria',
    note='The Bactrians and Sogdians, Iranian peoples and ancestors of the Tajiks, lived here, and both became Persian satrapies. The gold and silver Oxus Treasure, now in the British Museum, was found by the Amu Darya in 1877.'),
  P('Alexander and Greco-Bactria', -329, -130, '~Empire of Alexander', '~Seleucid Kingdom', '~Bactria',
    short='Greco-Bactria',
    note='Alexander the Great founded Alexandria Eschate, "the Furthest", at Khujand in 329 BC, and Greek kings of Bactria built the Temple of the Oxus at Takht-i Sangin.'),
  P('Kushan Empire', -130, 375, '~Kushan Empire', '~Yuezhi', short='Kushan',
    note='The Yuezhi conquered Bactria and founded the Kushan Empire, which spread Buddhism along the Silk Road.'),
  P('Hephthalites, Sogdians and Turks', 375, 710, '~Western Gokturk Khaganate', short='Sogdians',
    note='The Hephthalites and then the Western Turks ruled the land; Sogdian Panjakent was rich in painted houses, and the monastery of Ajina Tepa held a Buddha 13 metres long.'),
  P('Arab conquest', 710, 819, '~Umayyad Caliphate', '~Abbasid Caliphate', short='Arab conquest',
    note='Devashtich, the last Sogdian ruler of Panjakent, held out against the Arabs on Mount Mugh, where his letters were found in 1933, until he was taken and killed in 722.'),
  P('Samanid Empire', 819, 999, '~Samanid Empire', short='Samanid',
    note='Tajiks honour the Persian Samanids of Bukhara as their first state: the currency, the somoni, and the country\'s highest peak are named after Ismail Samani, and Rudaki, the father of Persian poetry, was born at Panjrud.'),
  P('Karakhanids to Khwarazmshahs', 999, 1220, '~Karkhanids', '~Ghaznavid Emirate', '~Kara Khitai Khaganate',
    '~Khwarazmian dynasty', short='Turkic dynasties',
    note='Turkic dynasties ruled, but Persian culture flourished; the poet Nasir Khusraw spread Ismaili Islam in the Pamirs, where Ismailis still honour him.'),
  P('Mongols and Timurids', 1220, 1500, '~Mongol Empire', '~Chagatai Khanate', '~Timurid Empire', '~Timurid Emirates',
    short='Mongols',
    note='Timur Malik held Khujand against Genghis Khan in 1220; Chagatai\'s khans and then Timur and his heirs ruled the land.'),
  P('Bukhara and Kokand', 1500, 1868, '~Bukara Khanate', '~Khiva Khanate', '~central Asian khanates', '~Turan',
    short='Bukhara',
    note='The Uzbek khans and emirs of Bukhara ruled the south, Kokand the north from the 1700s, and small mountain principalities such as Darvaz, Karategin and Badakhshan kept their own rulers.'),
  P('Russian Turkestan and the Emirate of Bukhara', 1868, 1920, '~Russian Empire', '~Bokhara Khanate',
    '~central Asian khanates', short='Russian',
    note='Russia took Khujand in 1866 and made the emir of Bukhara, who held the rest, its vassal in 1868; Russia and Britain divided the Pamirs in 1895. Dushanbe was a village named for its Monday market.'),
  P('Soviet Tajikistan', 1920, 1991, '~USSR', short='Soviet',
    note='The Red Army drove out the emir in 1920–21, and the Basmachi fought it in the mountains. Tajikistan became a Soviet republic in 1929, but Samarkand and Bukhara, centres of Tajik culture, were left in Uzbekistan; the Nurek dam was the tallest in the world.'),
  P('Republic of Tajikistan', 1991, None, 'Tajikistan', short='Republic',
    note='Independence in 1991 was followed by a civil war (1992–97) that killed tens of thousands of people; Emomali Rahmon has ruled since 1992.'),
)

# ---------- Oceania ----------

L('australia', 'Australia', 'Oceania / Islanders', (149.13, -35.28),
  P('First Australians', -10000, 1788, 'Aboriginal tribes', 'Australian aboriginal hunter-gatherers',
    'Aboriginal Tasmanians', 'Tasmanian hunter-gatherers', short='First Australians',
    note='People reached Australia more than 50,000 years ago, and their descendants make up the oldest living cultures on Earth, with more than 250 languages and the stories of the Dreaming. The Gunditjmara built stone eel traps at Budj Bim 6,600 years ago, and rising seas cut Tasmania off about 12,000 years ago.'),
  P('Convict colonies', 1788, 1851, 'New South Wales', short='Convicts',
    note='Arthur Phillip\'s First Fleet landed some 1,400 people, most of them convicts, at Sydney Cove on 26 January 1788; Britain sent 162,000 convicts in all. Smallpox and frontier wars killed many thousands of Aboriginal people as settlers spread inland, and new colonies were founded in Tasmania, Western Australia and South Australia.'),
  P('Gold rushes and self-governing colonies', 1851, 1901, 'New South Wales (UK)', 'Victoria (UK)', 'Queensland (UK)',
    'South Australia (UK)', 'Western Australia (UK)', 'Northern Territory (UK)', 'New South Wales',
    '~Australian aboriginal hunter-gatherers', short='Colonies',
    note='Gold was found in 1851, and Victoria\'s gold rush made Melbourne one of the richest cities in the world; miners rebelled at the Eureka Stockade in 1854. The six colonies governed themselves, and South Australia gave women the vote in 1894.'),
  P('Federation and the world wars', 1901, 1945, 'Australia', short='Federation',
    note='The colonies united as the Commonwealth of Australia on 1 January 1901, with a "White Australia" policy against Asian migrants. The landing of the ANZACs at Gallipoli in 1915 became a founding legend; Canberra became the capital in 1927, and Japanese planes bombed Darwin in 1942.'),
  P('Modern Australia', 1945, None, 'Australia', short='Modern',
    note='Millions of migrants came from Europe and later Asia, and the White Australia policy was ended by 1973. A referendum in 1967 counted Aboriginal people in the census, the High Court\'s Mabo decision recognised native title in 1992, and the government apologised to the Stolen Generations in 2008.'),
)

L('new_zealand', 'New Zealand', 'Oceania / Islanders', (175.5, -38.5),
  P('Polynesian settlers', 1250, 1500, 'Maori', short='Settlement',
    note='Voyagers from eastern Polynesia, steering by the stars, reached Aotearoa around 1250–1300, the last large land on Earth to be settled. They hunted the giant flightless moa to extinction within about two centuries.'),
  P('Classic Māori', 1500, 1769, 'Maori', 'Maoris', short='Māori',
    note='Māori lived in tribes (iwi), built hilltop forts (pā), carved meeting houses and worked greenstone; in 1642 they drove off Abel Tasman\'s Dutch ships at Golden Bay.'),
  P('Cook, traders and the Musket Wars', 1769, 1840, 'Māori', 'Maori', short='Musket Wars',
    note='James Cook charted the islands in 1769–70, and whalers, traders and missionaries followed. Tribes armed with muskets fought the Musket Wars (1807–42), which killed tens of thousands, and northern chiefs declared their independence in 1835.'),
  # the 1878 border file spells Māori with a question mark
  P('British colony', 1840, 1907, 'Māori', 'M?ori', short='Colony',
    note='More than 500 chiefs signed the Treaty of Waitangi with Britain in 1840, but wars over land (1845–72) and confiscations followed. Gold was found in Otago in 1861, and in 1893 New Zealand became the first self-governing country where women could vote.'),
  P('Dominion of New Zealand', 1907, 1947, 'New Zealand', short='Dominion',
    note='New Zealand became a dominion in 1907, and its ANZAC soldiers fought at Gallipoli in 1915; the first Labour government built a welfare state in the 1930s.'),
  P('Independent New Zealand', 1947, None, 'New Zealand', short='Independent',
    note='Full independence came in 1947; Edmund Hillary climbed Everest in 1953. New Zealand banned nuclear ships in the 1980s, and since 1975 the Waitangi Tribunal has heard Māori claims under the treaty.'),
)

L('papua_new_guinea', 'Papua New Guinea', 'Oceania / Islanders', (144.23, -5.86),
  P('First farmers', -8000, -1500, short='First farmers',
    note='People reached New Guinea more than 50,000 years ago, when it was joined to Australia. At Kuk Swamp in the highlands they drained the land to grow bananas and taro thousands of years ago, one of the few places on Earth where farming was invented on its own.'),
  P('Papuans and Lapita voyagers', -1500, 1884, 'Papuan neolithic farmers', 'Papuan', 'Papuans', 'Papua New Guinea',
    short='Papuans',
    note='The Lapita people, ancestors of the Polynesians, made their stamped pottery in the Bismarck islands from about 1500 BC. Hundreds of peoples speaking more than 800 languages lived in villages of farmers and traders, such as the Trobriand islanders of the Kula ring, and the sweet potato let the highland valleys fill with people.'),
  P('German and British New Guinea', 1884, 1914, 'Papua New Guinea', short='German & British',
    note='In 1884 Germany took the northeast and the Bismarck islands, and Britain the southeast; the Dutch had claimed the western half of the island in 1828. Australia took over British New Guinea, as Papua, in 1906.'),
  P('Australian rule', 1914, 1975, 'Papua New Guinea', '~Australia', short='Australian',
    note='Australia seized German New Guinea in 1914. Gold prospectors entering the highlands in the 1930s found about a million people unknown to the outside world; Japan invaded in 1942, and Australians fought it back along the Kokoda Track.'),
  P('Independent Papua New Guinea', 1975, None, 'Papua New Guinea', short='Independent',
    note='Papua New Guinea became independent in 1975 under Michael Somare. A war over the Panguna copper mine on Bougainville (1988–98) ended in autonomy, and the island voted for independence in 2019.'),
)

L('hawaii', 'Hawaii', 'Oceania / Islanders', (-155.5, 19.6),
  P('Polynesian settlers', 1000, 1400, 'Polynesians', short='Settlement',
    note='Voyagers in double-hulled canoes, steering by the stars, reached Hawaii from the Marquesas and Tahiti around 1000–1200, bringing taro, sweet potatoes, pigs and dogs. They built fishponds and stone temples (heiau).'),
  P('Ruling chiefs', 1400, 1782, 'Polynesians', 'Kō Hawaiʻi Paeʻāina (Hawaiian Kingdom)', short='Chiefdoms',
    note='Each island was ruled by chiefs (aliʻi) under the strict laws of kapu; ʻUmi-a-Līloa united the island of Hawaiʻi around 1500. James Cook reached the islands in 1778 and was killed at Kealakekua Bay the next year.'),
  P('Kamehameha unites the islands', 1782, 1810, 'Polynesians', short='Unification',
    note='Kamehameha, a chief of the island of Hawaiʻi, won his first battle in 1782, took Maui and Oʻahu, where his enemies were driven over the Nuʻuanu cliffs in 1795, and became king of all the islands when Kauaʻi submitted in 1810.'),
  P('Kingdom of Hawaii', 1810, 1893, 'Kingdom of Hawaii', short='Kingdom',
    note='Kamehameha\'s heirs abolished the kapu in 1819, welcomed American missionaries and gave the kingdom a constitution in 1840; sugar planters brought workers from China, Japan and Portugal. Diseases brought by foreigners killed most Native Hawaiians.'),
  P('Overthrow and annexation', 1893, 1898, 'Kingdom of Hawaii', short='Annexation',
    note='American planters, backed by US Marines, overthrew Queen Liliʻuokalani in 1893, and the United States annexed their Republic of Hawaii in 1898.'),
  P('Territory of Hawaii', 1898, 1959, '~United States', short='Territory',
    note='Hawaii was an American territory; Japan\'s attack on Pearl Harbor on 7 December 1941 brought the United States into the Second World War.'),
  P('State of Hawaii', 1959, None, '~United States', short='State',
    note='Hawaii became the 50th state in 1959, and tourism took the place of sugar; the Hawaiian language, nearly lost, was brought back in schools from the 1980s. Barack Obama, born in Honolulu in 1961, was the first president from Hawaii.'),
)

L('fiji', 'Fiji', 'Oceania / Islanders', (178.0, -17.8),
  P('Lapita settlers and chiefdoms', -1100, 1800, '~Tuʻi Tonga Empire', short='Chiefdoms',
    note='Lapita voyagers settled Fiji around 1000 BC, and later migrants from Melanesia mixed with them. Fijians lived in warring chiefdoms, built great canoes and traded with and fought the Tongans to the east.'),
  P('Cakobau and the Tongan wars', 1800, 1874, '~Tuʻi Tonga Empire', short='Cakobau',
    note='Traders came for sandalwood and sea cucumbers; Cakobau, chief of the little island of Bau, rose to power, while the Tongan prince Enele Maʻafu took the Lau islands. Cakobau was proclaimed king of Fiji in 1871.'),
  P('British colony', 1874, 1970, 'Fiji', short='Colony',
    note='Cakobau handed Fiji to Britain in 1874, and a measles epidemic killed a third of the Fijians the next year; from 1879 Britain brought 60,000 Indian labourers to work the sugar fields, and their descendants became almost half the people.'),
  P('Independent Fiji', 1970, None, 'Fiji', short='Independent',
    note='Fiji became independent in 1970. Two coups in 1987 and another in 2000 were made in the name of indigenous Fijians against governments backed by Indo-Fijians, and the army seized power again in 2006.'),
)

L('tonga', 'Tonga', 'Oceania / Islanders', (-175.2, -21.1),
  P('Lapita settlers', -900, 950, short='Lapita',
    note='Lapita voyagers settled Tonga around 900 BC; from here and Samoa their descendants, the Polynesians, went on to settle the Pacific.'),
  P('Tuʻi Tonga Empire', 950, 1799, 'Tuʻi Tonga Empire', short='Tuʻi Tonga',
    note='The sacred kings (Tuʻi Tonga), said to descend from the god Tangaloa, ruled from Muʻa, where their stone tombs still stand, and raised the trilithon of Haʻamonga ʻa Maui. Their seafaring power reached Samoa, Fiji and ʻUvea; James Cook called Tonga the Friendly Islands in 1773.'),
  P('Civil wars', 1799, 1845, short='Civil wars',
    note='The chiefs fought a long civil war, while Methodist missionaries won converts.'),
  P('Kingdom of Tonga', 1845, 1970, 'Tonga', short='Kingdom',
    note='Tāufaʻāhau, baptised George, united the islands as King George Tupou I in 1845 and gave them a constitution in 1875 that is still in force. Tonga was a British protected state from 1900 but was never a colony; Queen Sālote rode through the rain in an open carriage at Elizabeth II\'s coronation in 1953.'),
  P('Independent Tonga', 1970, None, 'Tonga', short='Independent',
    note='Tonga regained full independence in 1970 and became a more democratic monarchy in 2010; the huge undersea eruption of Hunga Tonga–Hunga Haʻapai in 2022 was heard around the world.'),
)

L('samoa', 'Samoa', 'Oceania / Islanders', (-171.8, -13.9),
  P('Lapita settlers and Tongan rule', -800, 1250, '~Tuʻi Tonga Empire', short='Lapita',
    note='Lapita voyagers settled Samoa around 800 BC. Tongan kings ruled the islands for centuries until, Samoans say, the brothers Tuna and Fata drove them out and founded the Malietoa title.'),
  P('Paramount chiefs', 1250, 1830, short='Chiefs',
    note='High chiefs held the great titles of the islands; around 1500 Queen Salamasina held all four of them at once.'),
  P('Missionaries and the rival powers', 1830, 1900, 'Samoa', short='Rival powers',
    note='John Williams brought Christianity in 1830. Germany, Britain and the United States backed rival chiefs, and German and American warships were wrecked in a cyclone at Apia in 1889; the writer Robert Louis Stevenson lived and died there.'),
  P('German and New Zealand Samoa', 1900, 1962, 'Samoa', short='Colonial',
    note='In 1899 the powers split the islands: Germany took the west and the United States the east (American Samoa). New Zealand seized German Samoa in 1914; the 1918 flu killed a fifth of the people, and New Zealand police shot the peaceful Mau independence movement\'s leader in 1929.'),
  P('Independent Samoa', 1962, None, 'Samoa', short='Independent',
    note='Samoa became the first Pacific island country to win independence, in 1962.'),
)
